% This is a placeholder for the thermal response Simulink subsystem.
% In a real MATLAB/Simulink environment, this would be a graphical subsystem
% implementing the thermal response model.

% The subsystem should model:
% - Temperature rise from joule heating during excitation
% - Rate of temperature change (dT/dt)
%
% Inputs:
% - Excitation pulse (current squared for power calculation)
% - Degradation mode parameters (thermal resistance, capacitance, heat factor)
% - Ambient temperature
%
% Outputs:
% - Temperature rise over time
% - dT/dt (rate of temperature change)

% The thermal response can be modeled as a simple thermal mass:
% C_th * dT/dt = P_heat - (T - T_ambient)/R_th
%
% Where:
% - C_th is thermal capacitance (J/K)
% - R_th is thermal resistance (K/W)
% - P_heat is power dissipated as heat (W)
% - T is temperature (K)
% - T_ambient is ambient temperature (K)
% - dT/dt is rate of temperature change (K/s)
%
% P_heat = I^2 * R0_eff
% where R0_eff = R0_nominal * R0_scale (from degradation mode)
%
% With degradation mode scaling:
% - R_th_eff = R_th_nominal * R_th_scale
% - C_th_eff = C_th_nominal * C_th_scale
% - heat_factor scales the power dissipation

% This file represents the intended structure. Actual implementation requires
% using MATLAB Simulink editor to create the graphical subsystem with:
% - Gain blocks for resistance and capacitance scaling
% - Integrator blocks for thermal dynamics
% - Math operations for power calculation and heat transfer
% - Parameter references to workspace or data dictionary